@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
' SPEC source (every file that cites an id):
'   cmd/proveo/main.go · cmd/proveo/main_test.go — C6, D2
'   internal/egress/plan.go · internal/egress/egress_test.go — D1, D3, C1
'   internal/workspace/mount.go · internal/workspace/mount_test.go — D4, D5, D6
title Design decision ids — recovered from the source that cites them

node "D-series — structural decisions\nwhere a thing lives and how it is extended" as DS <<host>> {
  component "**D1**\none definition, no parallel list" as D1 <<app>>
  component "**D2**\npure seams, testable without Docker" as D2 <<app>>
  component "**D3**\na variant is one table entry" as D3 <<app>>
  component "**D4**\nrunner formats argv, it does not decide" as D4 <<app>>
  component "**D5**\nthe manifest owns a shape" as D5 <<app>>
  component "**D6**\nthe mount model is declared, not coded" as D6 <<app>>
}

node "C-series — runtime constraints\nwhat must hold while a run executes" as CS <<host>> {
  component "**C1**\noperator domains are honoured unconditionally" as C1 <<app>>
  component "**C2**\nthe injected secret does not outlive the run" as C2 <<app>>
  component "**C4**\nteardown survives signals" as C4 <<app>>
  component "**C6**\nthe agent's exit code passes through" as C6 <<app>>
  component "**C7**\nstage only what the plan needs" as C7 <<app>>
}

component "An id cited by code but defined nowhere\nreads as a promise someone else already checked" as DEAD <<error>>
database "This diagram — the definitions,\nderived from the citing prose" as DEFS <<db>>

DS ARROW_MAIN DEFS
CS ARROW_MAIN DEFS
DEFS ARROW_ERROR DEAD : what these citations were until now

note bottom of DEAD
  **Provenance, stated plainly.** These ids are cited in eleven places
  across ""cmd/proveo"", ""internal/egress"" and ""internal/workspace"", and they
  were **never defined anywhere in this repository** — not in ""_spec"", not
  in any ""\.md"", and not even in the commit that introduced them
  (""feat: go migration & upgrades""). They arrived from a design
  conversation that never landed.

  So the source is the only authority, and every definition below is
  //derived from the prose that cites it//. That prose is quoted closely
  rather than re-imagined: an id whose meaning is reconstructed loosely is
  worse than no id at all, because it silently re-labels a decision
  nobody made.

  Four ids —​ **D2 · C2 · C4 · C7** —​ were cited by prose that has since
  moved into named diagrams, which now state those facts without an id.
  Their entries here exist so an old citation, a commit message or a
  reviewer's memory still resolves.

end note

note right of D1
  **Every created resource is paired with its teardown at the moment it is
  added, and shared wiring is written once.**

  ""builder"" records each network and sidecar together with the command
  that removes it, instead of a second hand-synced cleanup list — the
  original form was a silent container-leak hazard, because forgetting an
  entry produced no error at all.

  Same principle on the other side: ""attachLocalModel"" is shared by all
  three modes and ""localModelArgs"" is one superset each harness consumes
  selectively, so the local-model wiring cannot drift per mode or per
  agent. //Cited at// ""internal/egress/plan\.go"" ×3.
end note

note right of D2
  **The gating, dispatch and assembly logic is extracted into pure seams.**
  ""assemble"" reads only resolved inputs — no environment, filesystem or
  exec — so a topology and an agent run config can be asserted with no
  Docker present.

  The test file names the motivation directly: these are "the seams that
  were untestable inside the old god-function". //Cited at//
  ""cmd/proveo/main_test\.go"", and by the ""assemble"" prose now held in
  ""_spec/internal/egress/teardown-and-signals\.puml"".
end note

note right of D3
  **Adding an egress mode is a one-line entry.** ""modeBuilders"" is the
  single source of both the canonical order (CLI help and validation) and
  the plan builder (dispatch), so a new mode cannot be half-added — present
  in the switch but missing from help, or vice versa.
  //Cited at// ""internal/egress/plan\.go"".
end note

note right of D4
  **""internal/runner"" stays a pure argv formatter.** Mount planning lives
  in ""internal/workspace"", beside the git-scope resolver that decides what
  to mount — not in the package that renders flags. Execution is the
  caller's concern, which is what keeps the argv golden-testable.
  //Cited at// ""internal/workspace/mount\.go"".
end note

note right of D5
  **A shape declared in the manifest is embedded, never re-declared.**
  ""MountSpec"" embeds ""manifest\.Workspace"" rather than copying its fields,
  so the manifest stays the single source of that shape and a schema change
  cannot leave two definitions disagreeing.
  //Cited at// ""internal/workspace/mount\.go"".
end note

note right of D6
  **The workspace mount model is declared per def, not decided in code** —
  including ""mode: ro"", which is what makes an ""/app"" mount read-only. A
  harness changes its mount posture by editing its manifest, not by
  reaching into mount planning. //Cited at//
  ""internal/workspace/mount_test\.go"".
end note

note left of C1
  **Operator-supplied domains are honoured whether or not a provider is
  pinned.** The regression this guards: an early return dropped custom
  domains entirely when no named provider was detected, so a run with only
  ""\.myhost\.internal"" got an allowlist that admitted nothing.
  //Cited at// ""internal/egress/egress_test\.go"".
end note

note left of C2
  **The injected credential must not outlive the run.** The broker env file
  is removed on every teardown path, including the signal path.
  Enforcement now described in
  ""_spec/internal/egress/teardown-and-signals\.puml"".
end note

note left of C4
  **Teardown survives SIGINT and SIGTERM.** Go does not run deferred
  functions when a signal ends the process, so the topology, the secret and
  any privileged sidecar come down from a signal handler instead.
  Enforcement now described in
  ""_spec/internal/egress/teardown-and-signals\.puml"".
end note

note left of C6
  **The agent's own non-zero exit is not a proveo error.** Its code
  propagates verbatim, with no ""error:"" prefix, so a harness's exit status
  survives the wrapper.

  Only the agent's. A failed helper subprocess — ""docker pull"", a
  ""build\.sh"" — also wraps an ""*exec\.ExitError"", and treating that as an
  agent exit would end the run silently. A named exit type is what keeps
  the two apart. //Cited at// ""cmd/proveo/main\.go"" and its regression test.
end note

note left of C7
  **Only what the plan needs is staged.** Squid config and log dir when
  there is a Squid sidecar, the mitmproxy confdir and flow dir only on the
  firewall tier. Enforcement now described in
  ""_spec/internal/egress/teardown-and-signals\.puml"".
end note
@enduml
